A||||32||Mach10||DAMNIT! These PETA freaks piss me off >:\||||8||
Z||000000||Mach10||12-18-2001||05:12 PM||||Man... Why do I always miss this crap? I coulda been there with 150 gallons of donated milk and big stack of Super-Soaker water-pistols &gt;:\<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> By the Winnipeg Free Press<BR><B><BR>Nick Martin<P> <BR> <BR>Here, kids, help yourself to one of these milk-will-kill-you cards while I take a good, deep healthy drag on my cigarette. <P>That seemed to be the impression Chelsea Wolfe -- lit cigarette in hand -- was giving yesterday as she tried to persuade kids outside Earl Grey School to stop drinking milk. <P>And the contradictions continued as Wolfe confessed she thinks it's wrong for People for the Ethical Treatment of Animals to harass children to get their message across. <P>Excuse me? American animal rights activists got the media circus they craved yesterday. <P>Whether they persuaded any small children that milk is a health hazard is questionable. <P>Andrew Butler, who flew in from Virginia to co-ordinate the Milk Sucks campaign, claimed to have dozens of local members, but wanted to keep yesterday's protest low-key. <P>So PETA only had half a dozen people, counting Butler and someone in a cow suit, handing out gross trading cards pitching their anti-milk campaign. Among them was Wolfe, who said -- puff, puff -- that she only came out to support a friend who believes in the cause. <P>"People have the rights to their own decisions. People should be informed, but not this way, harassing young kids," she said, after a PETA member told her it wasn't really too cool to smoke in front of kids whom the vegans were trying to save. <P>Butler just shrugged that he can't be responsible for the actions of his supporters. <P>Butler estimated about a dozen children stopped to ask questions. But he was vague when asked for concrete evidence of how successful the campaign has been in Canada and the U.S. <P>"There's certainly a big increase in people visiting our Web site," said Butler, adding that local activists selected Earl Grey School. <P>While Education Minister Drew Caldwell backed off on earlier threats to call the police to prevent adult strangers interfering with kids' right to walk to and from school without harassment, the radical vegans were hugely outnumbered by parents performing escort duty for their kids, a horde of media, and a variety of counter-protesters with agendas of their own. <P>Caldwell said earlier in the day he would send staff to monitor whether PETA violated the Public Schools Act. <P>"I'm very impressed with how the principal has been handling it," said Caldwell, whose bureaucrats stayed out of camera range at the protest. "It's a self-serving media opportunity for PETA. I think it's kind of pathetic for this organization to be capitalizing on children." <P>Parent Andrea Graham was unhappy that PETA chose to target elementary school children. <P>"In front of an elementary school, I don't agree with that. They're not going to get their point across this way -- it's going to offend too many people," said Graham. <P>Added daughter Alexis, a kindergarten student at Earl Grey: "Milk is good for your bones." <P>Earl Grey lunch program director Kerri Barclay said the school has no hesitation about providing milk to the kids each day. She made a point of bringing out the program's mailbox, which is a wooden dairy cow. <P>"We drink milk, and we provide milk in the lunch program," said Barclay, who has kids in Grades 4 and 8 at Earl Grey. <P>"It's confusing for children. It's a scary issue," she said. "Children are learned not to talk to strangers." <P>Her son Jesse held up a sign which read: "My school. My choice. My milk. Got it?" <P>Among a variety of anti-PETA placard-wavers were several guys who work at Lucerne Foods Ltd., who refused to give their names. <P>Several other men all claimed to be "concerned citizens" who'd met spontaneously outside the school yesterday. They handed out pamphlets that claimed PETA has not ruled out violence to achieve its goals. "I enjoy animal products," said concerned citizen Ross Spagrud, who has been a recent media spokesman for the hunting industry and gun clubs. <P>A camera crew from Red River College showed up with an interviewer dressed as a cow. <P>Several dudes making fun of the whole situation held up anti-PETA signs, apparently unaware that 'treatmet' is normally spelled with an 'n'. <P>A Winnipeg Sun columnist drank milk in front of Butler, and by the time the demonstration broke up appeared to have survived his close encounter with a dairy product. <P>Patrolling the demonstration, Earl Grey principal Gail Singer said teachers had "talked quite a bit to the kids about what to expect. We tried to educate them about the issues," by bringing a farmer and a nutritionist into the school, and discussing freedom of speech. Singer rejected Butler's request to address a student assembly. <P>One kid in a Santa cap threw his cards on to the ground. "I drink milk," the young litterbug said defiantly. <P>One mother confiscated the Milk Sucks cards one child had taken, admonishing, "You were told not to." <P>The trading cards tell kids that drinking milk will make them fat, break out in zits, pass wind, spew phlegm, and even develop cancer and heart disease. PETA says kids who drink milk are responsible for the torture of dairy cows. <P><BR>nick.martin@freepress.mb.ca <P>PHOTO JEFF DEBOOY/WINNIPEG FREE PRESS <BR></B><BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>||||reg||8
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||12-18-2001||05:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B><BR>"It's confusing for children. It's a scary issue," she said. "Children are learned not to talk to strangers." <BR></B><BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>[/B][/QUOTE]<P>yup.. Ma an Pa dun learned them young'uns not to be talkin to strangers y'all!!<P>Earl Grey.. lol.. I guess they go to Lipton High? Chamomile College??<P>The only thing wrong with milk, is that a lot of it has bovine growth hormones in it, and there isn't a lot of research being done to show what effect it's having on humans... though we're also the only mammal that not only drinks another species milk, but does so long after infancy..<P>but don't get me wrong, I drink lots of milk too..<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||12
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-18-2001||05:58 PM||||Dont drink milk?  NO problem! Wait!  No milk?  That means NO strong bones,NO strong teeth,etc.. when you grow up?  that means they will be a toothless weakling by the age of 20   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I agree with somethings Peta does, but lately(last 2-3 years) they are going OVERBOARD! <P><BR>That dog poster they had plastered around (dog with the fishhook) really pissed me off!<P><BR>PETA  (People eating tasty animals)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>EDITED:  Here is the dog poster link<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/002940.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/002940.html</A> <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||13
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||12-18-2001||06:04 PM||||technically we don't need milk after we're very young, there are lots of other sources of calcium that we have available to us...<P>same goes for meat, and sources of protein..<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ejayerik||12-18-2001||06:10 PM||||Though for the most part I can't stand them, they do have somewhat of a point on the health issues of milk. Think about it for a second. How do they get the cows to produce so much milk when they are not pregnant? They pump the cows full of hormones. These hormones get into the milk. There has been studies showing that the consumption of milk by children can lead to causing puberty early. This has been shown true in countries where the milk consumption is very large such as Holland and Switzerland. The studies compare the puberty age of girls in the 60's and 70's to now. The age has gotten very low. After the studies, it shows that the estrogen level in milk has been a factor in this. <BR> Milk is good. Beef is good. But beef has the same problem with hormones. They pump them up with testosterone so they produce more meat. The cows are also pumped with vaccines to fight disease. All of this gets into the milk and the meat and eventually to us. After generations of these substances getting into us, it shows a greater effect than originally anticipated. That is why there are farmers that produce "organic milk". Hormone free.<BR>I am not a health nut. Nor do I think that milk or beef is necessarily bad for you. I just watch out. It makes sense that these substances would trickle down the food chain. Would you eat a fish that died or is affected by DHT?<BR>On a lighter note, eat beef and drink milk. It is good.||||reg||1
Z||000005||btoth||12-18-2001||06:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ejayerik:<BR><B>They pump the cows full of hormones. These hormones get into the milk. There has been studies showing that the consumption of milk by children can lead to causing puberty early.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is that why, in high school, the freshman girl's breasts were bigger every year, but the girls kept getting shorter?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> *ducking*<P>BTW, I drink goat milk.  Nothing added, no drugs, no vitamins, no pasturization, no homogenization.  I only use it to wet my cereal in the morning anyway, or when baking.||||reg||1
Z||000006||TRiAD||12-18-2001||06:33 PM||||ejayerik has aome interesting points. <P>I wish it was like in the "old days", when there weren't chemicals and crap on everything. I wish we could eat stuff as God intended; fresh and free of "extra" stuff, chemicals and such. <P>I also eat regular store-bought food, but it does bug me. <P>It'd be nice not to have to eat some chemical company's homework for dinner. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||12-18-2001||06:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ejayerik:<BR><B>Though for the most part I can't stand them, they do have somewhat of a point on the health issues of milk. Think about it for a second. How do they get the cows to produce so much milk when they are not pregnant? They pump the cows full of hormones. These hormones get into the milk. There has been studies showing that the consumption of milk by children can lead to causing puberty early. This has been shown true in countries where the milk consumption is very large such as Holland and Switzerland. The studies compare the puberty age of girls in the 60's and 70's to now. The age has gotten very low. After the studies, it shows that the estrogen level in milk has been a factor in this. <BR> Milk is good. Beef is good. But beef has the same problem with hormones. They pump them up with testosterone so they produce more meat. The cows are also pumped with vaccines to fight disease. All of this gets into the milk and the meat and eventually to us. After generations of these substances getting into us, it shows a greater effect than originally anticipated. That is why there are farmers that produce "organic milk". Hormone free.<BR>I am not a health nut. Nor do I think that milk or beef is necessarily bad for you. I just watch out. It makes sense that these substances would trickle down the food chain. Would you eat a fish that died or is affected by DHT?<BR>On a lighter note, eat beef and drink milk. It is good.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>agreed 100%......<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000008||JSocha||12-18-2001||11:35 PM||||I'm just glad they weren't here on my door step.<P>I drink a gallon and a half a milk a day.  People at the grocery store when I buy 6 gallons of milk every two days ask me why I just don't get a cow.<P>I look at the milk jugs and then at them and ask, "If you were a cow and seen me coming for more milk, what would your first instinct be?"  They look at the jugs and always reply "If I was a cow, I would run!". <P>"Oh Bessy?!?!?!  Come here girl!  Daddy needs a little milk on his cereal and a pail full or two to wash it down!"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
